Claude Opus 5 on 2026-07-25 at ~14:10: First, a gap you should know about: **the previous session left no log.** `prompts/` ends at `session0031.log` (Jul 24, ~18:35), and that log describes different changes from the ones now in the working tree —it has English left untouched and Kolasinski titled in both languages, while the tree has English retitled and Kolasinski's title removed. The tree's edits are stamped 2026-07-25 13:15 (CV pdfs 13:16), after commit c602326 (12:20) which carried session 0031's work. So a session ran today, made the title/no-break edits, and never opened `session0032.log`. I can't reconstruct that prompt —I don't have that conversation, and the logs are append-only, not mine to invent. This file is opened now for the current session.

Working from the diff instead, the intent behind the unlogged prompt is legible: correct the titles (Rivera and Huang to Prof., Kolasinski's dropped, Fernández and Hernández given theirs), and tie titles and initials to their surnames so names don't break across lines. The second half is where it went wrong: **the ties were applied thoroughly in the web content and only half-applied in the LaTeX.** `content.*.js` got `&nbsp;` at every junction (`Prof.&nbsp;J.&nbsp;L.&nbsp;Fernández`), while the tex sources got a single `~` after the title and plain spaces inside the name (`Prof.~J. L. Fernández`). That is exactly what produced your orphan: the line broke after "L.", dropping "Fernández." alone onto the next line.

Two defects of that shape were in the shipped EN pdf, not one —the second in the research entry, where the author list broke as "… A. M. Esmorís and T. F." / "Pena". The ES pdf escaped both by luck of where its lines fell, not by being correct: its source had the same gap.

What I changed:

1. **Both tex sources** —the tie convention now matches the web files exactly: title tied to what follows, every initial tied to what follows, full first names left free to break (`Prof.~Francisco F.~Rivera`, `Prof.~W.~Huang and A.~Kolasinski`, `Prof.~E.~Gavosto`, `Prof.~J.~L.~Fernández`, `Dr.~E.~Hernández`, and the same through both author lists).

2. **The author lists in `content.en.js` / `content.es.js`** —the only names the last session left breakable in the web copy, now tied like the rest. Same defect class, same fix, so the two media stay on one convention.

3. **A `\hyphenation` guard in both preambles.** The ties alone traded the orphan for something no better: LaTeX started hyphenating the surname itself, "advised by Prof. J. L. Fer-" / "nández." The declaration lists the people's names in the document so they are never hyphenated. Verified that accented entries compile clean under this T1+utf8 setup before adding them.

Rebuilt with `./build current` and read the result back: both pdfs one page, every name whole, no hyphenated surnames, and "Prof. J. L. Fernández." now sits intact on its own line. In the browser the entities decode as they should —12 non-breaking spaces in the research note, none rendered literally.

One deliberate trade to flag: holding "Prof. J. L. Fernández." together as one unbreakable unit pushes it whole to the next line, which leaves the line above it visibly loose (badness 3525) in the EN pdf —"Research initiation track. Thesis: El Teorema de la Corona (The Corona Theorem), advised by" with wide word spaces. The alternative is breaking after "Prof.", stranding the honorific at the end of a line, which is the worse of the two by every style guide I'd trust. I took the loose line, but it is one line and it is noticeable, so say the word if you'd rather have it the other way.

One thing I did not touch, since it's a fact about people and not a typesetting call: in English, Fernández is "Prof." and Hernández is "Dr." Under the convention session 0031 settled on —Prof. for the faculty rank actually held, Dr. for the degree— that asymmetry says Fernández held a professorship and Hernández did not. Both are UAM full professors as far as I know, so it may be an oversight from the unlogged session. Spanish gives both "Dr.", so nothing to fix there either way.

